---
name: minimize-context
description: >
  How to minimize the main-thread context — shed spent context at natural seams so
  the working window holds only what the next decision needs. Covers what a "seam"
  is, the write-to-disk-then-compact discipline, the real mechanics (/compact,
  /clear, /context, CLAUDE.md compact instructions, auto-compaction), and the
  over-minning failure mode. Use when a conversation is getting long, after a plan
  or finding is settled, or whenever you want to deliberately reduce context. The
  context-reduction half of contextminning-subagentmaxxing; pairs with subagent-launcher.
---

# How to Minimize Context

Keeping the main-thread context lean is the single highest-leverage habit on a long
task. This skill is the *how* of the reduction side. (For delegating verbose work to
a different model so it never enters context in the first place, see the
`subagent-launcher` skill — the two are complementary halves.)

## Why bother

Context is the working memory of the thread. Everything in it is re-read on every
turn — so a long context is slower, more expensive, and, past a point, *worse at the
task*: the signal you need gets buried under the trail of how you got there. Models
attend less reliably across very long contexts, and detail from early instructions
degrades through compaction.

So the real asset isn't "how much have we discussed" — it's "how much of what's
present right now is load-bearing for the next decision."

The mental model: **the main thread is a workbench, not a warehouse.** Finished work
goes on a shelf (a file). Heavy fabrication happens in another room (a subagent — see
`subagent-launcher`). The bench stays clear enough to think on.

## Minimize at the seams, never on a timer

The instinct to avoid is compacting *because the context is getting long*. That's
how you lose work: you compact mid-thought, the summary drops the nuance you were
about to use, and you pay to rediscover it. Reduction is about **seams**, not size.

A seam is a point where a unit of work has become *durable and done*:

- A plan or spec has been argued out and **written to a markdown file.** The
  argument is now redundant with the artifact — shed it.
- A long file, log, or search was read to extract *one* fact or decision. Keep the
  fact; the raw material is dead weight.
- A subtask was completed **and verified.** The outcome matters; the keystrokes
  don't.
- A line of investigation was *ruled out.* "We checked X, it's not the cause" is one
  sentence worth keeping; the investigation behind it is not.

The discipline is: **write the durable thing down first, then compact.** You can only
safely forget what you can re-read. If the conclusion isn't in a file (or isn't going
to survive in the compaction summary), you haven't earned the right to shed it yet.

**Prefer a megaplan asset for real work.** A loose `PLAN.md` is the minimum; better is
to land the spec as a **megaplan starting/plan file** or a **megaplan ticket** (see the
`megaplan` and `megaplan-tickets` skills). That way the thing you shed from context
doesn't just sit in a note — it becomes an executable, trackable unit of work that
survives the compaction and can be picked up later. The seam where a project gets
spec'd out is the natural moment to create that asset, then compact from there.

## Mechanics that actually exist

- `/compact` — summarizes the conversation in place, same session. Takes optional
  focus text: `/compact keep the schema decisions and the open questions`. Older
  conversation is retrievable via `/resume`.
- A **"Compact Instructions"** section in `CLAUDE.md` steers what auto-compaction
  preserves — set it for a project where certain facts must always survive.
- **Auto-compaction** fires around ~95% capacity on its own: it clears old tool
  outputs first, then summarizes. You want to compact at clean seams *before* this,
  so the summary is shaped by you, not by an emergency at 95%.
- `/clear` — starts a fresh conversation (old one stays in `/resume`). Right when the
  *next* task is unrelated to this one. Different from `/compact`, which continues
  the same thread.
- `/context` — shows where the tokens currently are, broken down by category
  (system, tools, MCP, memory, skills, messages). Use it to find what to shed.
- `/context-usage` — the *trajectory* `/context` can't show: reads the on-disk
  session transcript and plots context size turn-by-turn, marking every compaction
  boundary (auto/manual, with before→after token counts on Claude). Use it to see
  *how* context grew, whether you're minning at the right seams, and when
  auto-compaction has already fired. Works on Codex sessions too (`--codex`).

## The over-minning failure mode

Compacting too eagerly is its own slop. Symptoms: re-reading files you already read,
re-deriving decisions, asking the user something they already answered. If you find
yourself reconstructing recent context, you compacted too soon or too aggressively.
When in doubt, leave it — a slightly long context costs less than a lost decision.

## Pairs with subagent-launcher

Reduction sheds the scaffolding of work that's *already in* context. The other lever
is preventing verbose work from ever entering: hand research, validation, and wide
searches to a subagent (DeepSeek/Kimi/Codex via `subagent-launcher`) so only the
conclusion returns. A healthy long task uses both — delegate the byproduct-heavy
work outward, and compact the durable conclusions inward. The umbrella skill that
runs both as one posture is `contextminning-subagentmaxxing`.
